# .uis.extend/dagster-code-locations.yaml
#
# WHICH APPLICATIONS THIS INSTALLATION ORCHESTRATES WITH DAGSTER.
#
# UIS ships the orchestrator; applications register themselves here. This file
# is per-installation and is NOT part of the UIS product — the same way
# prometheus-targets.yaml holds this installation's scrape targets.
#
# ⚠️ An application's own installer should write its entry here. Editing the
# platform's manifests/360-dagster-config.yaml instead would put one tenant's
# configuration inside the product, and the next installation would inherit it.
#
# A "code location" is a container image the application publishes; Dagster
# loads its asset definitions over gRPC. dagster-pipes means the work itself can
# be in any language — the image only has to speak the gRPC handshake.
#
# ⚠️ WHAT THE IMAGE MUST CONTAIN — the full contract is in
#    /docs/services/analytics/dagster, but one requirement is worth repeating
#    here because its absence is invisible:
#
#      the image MUST be able to `import dagster_postgres.run_storage`
#      (pip install dagster-postgres — 0.29.x for the 1.13.x line)
#
#    Run pods are launched from YOUR image and this platform stores runs in
#    Postgres. Without that module the location still loads, reports LOADED,
#    serves its assets and schedules, and sits at 0 restarts — and then every
#    run dies in ~5 seconds having executed zero steps.
#
#    `./uis verify dagster` pre-flights this (check D5). It caught the first real
#    tenant, which met every other requirement.
#
# After changing this file:  ./uis deploy dagster
#
# Remove or comment out everything below for an installation with no tenants;
# an empty file and a missing file are both fine.

code_locations: []

# Worked example — an application called `atlas`:
#
# code_locations:
#   - name: atlas-data
#     image: ghcr.io/terchris/atlas-data
#     tag: v20260822-abc1234        # NEVER `latest` — see below
#     digest: sha256:d7a371f7...    # optional, and the only real pin — see below
#     module: atlas_data.definitions
#     why: "Ingests 41 Norwegian public-data sources, then dbt builds marts.* and api_v1.*"
#     env_secrets:
#       - atlas-database-url        # supplies ATLAS_DATABASE_URL to the pods
#
# ⚠️ THE SECRET'S KEYS BECOME THE ENV VARS, AND THERE ARE TWO WAYS TO CREATE ONE.
#    They do not produce the same names, and picking the wrong one is invisible
#    until a run pod fails to connect:
#
#      ./uis configure postgresql --app atlas \
#          --namespace dagster --secret-name-prefix atlas-database
#        -> Secret `atlas-database-db` (the prefix gains `-db`), key always
#           `DATABASE_URL`. Also creates the database and its owning role.
#
#      kubectl create secret generic atlas-database-url --namespace dagster \
#          --from-literal=ATLAS_DATABASE_URL=...
#        -> the name and the key are both yours. The database is NOT created.
#
#    The example above is the kubectl form. Full comparison:
#    /docs/services/analytics/dagster
#
# Fields:
#   name        required  the code-location name Dagster shows in its UI
#   image       required  repository, without the tag
#   tag         required  an immutable tag. `latest` is REJECTED: Helm needs the
#                         image field to CHANGE to roll the pod, so `latest`
#                         silently keeps serving old code after a "successful"
#                         deploy
#   env         optional  plain environment variables for the pod, written by
#                         `uis template install` from the application's own
#                         exports. Not hand-written in the normal case.
#
#    🔵 An application declares `env_from_exports` in its artifact as
#       NAME -> export key, and the installer resolves it:
#
#         code_location:
#           env_from_exports:
#             ATLAS_POSTGREST_URL: api-url
#
#       The value is whatever `exports.api-url` resolved to at install. Nothing
#       is restated — the definition already says what the URL is, once.
#
#    ⚠️ A named export that does not exist REFUSES the install. An env var set
#       to empty is not the same as unset and is worse: the check would run,
#       reach nothing, and have to guess why.
#
#   resources   optional  requests for the code-location pod. UIS sets a default
#                         of cpu 100m / memory 384Mi; declare this only if your
#                         image genuinely needs more.
#
#    🔴 WITHOUT A REQUEST A POD IS BestEffort: first evicted under node memory
#       pressure, first OOM-killed. The code-location pod is LONG-LIVED, so its
#       eviction takes your whole location offline — assets unqueryable, and the
#       symptom reads as "Dagster is broken" rather than "a job died".
#
#       Measured on a real tenant: the code-location pod peaked at 300 MiB while
#       reserving nothing, beside a platform daemon reserving 512 MiB and peaking
#       at 309. The unprotected pod used within 9 MiB of the protected one.
#
#    ⚠️ UIS sets requests and NOT limits, on purpose. A request is a floor and
#       getting it slightly low still protects you. A limit is a ceiling, and a
#       memory limit set below a real peak turns a working pipeline into a
#       reliable OOMKill. If you set limits here, you own that trade.
#
#       resources:
#         requests:
#           memory: 1Gi
#
#   digest      optional  the digest that tag is expected to resolve to. If set,
#                         the deploy resolves the tag at the registry and REFUSES
#                         if it has moved.
#
#    🔴 AN IMMUTABLE-LOOKING TAG IS NOT AN IMMUTABLE TAG. `v20260822-abc1234`
#       satisfies the `latest` rule above completely and can still be re-pushed:
#       nothing at a registry prevents it. The rule above is about Helm rolling
#       the pod — a DEPLOYMENT-CORRECTNESS argument. It is not an integrity one,
#       and it was the only one UIS had.
#
#       The install definition is pulled at a digest and checked against the pin
#       the catalogue records. Until now the CODE image — the thing that actually
#       executes — had no field to put a digest in, so an application could not
#       pin it even if it wanted to (imac via ops-dev, urb-agents#740).
#
#    ⚠️ Optional on purpose. An application that cannot publish digests must
#       still be installable. `./uis deploy dagster` PRINTS the resolved digest
#       for every code location whether or not one is declared, so the value to
#       paste in here is in the output of the deploy you just ran, and
#       `./uis verify dagster` reports the digest each location is actually
#       RUNNING (read from `imageID`, not from the tag we asked for).
#   module      required  python module exposing `definitions`
#   why         required  what this installation loses if it stops running.
#                         Same rule as prometheus-targets.yaml — a tenant nobody
#                         can justify is one nobody maintains
#   env_secrets optional  secrets in the `dagster` namespace to expose to the
#                         code-location pod and its run pods. Every key in the
#                         secret becomes an environment variable verbatim — see
#                         the warning above about which name you get
